\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces  General capability after suppression on PIQA with the diagonal Fisher at different step sizes. The suppression direction is calculated with PIQA examples only. Differences are measured in percentage points relative to the unedited perturbed model. }}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:piqa-capability}{{3}{6}{}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces  Membership inference results on PIQA. AUC is computed on each duplication level's members against all dup~$=0$ non-members. \textbf  {Projection} is a first-order log-likelihood ratio between the perturbed model and the suppressed model. Reference is an oracle method using the same ratio but with the standard rather than suppressed model. \textbf  {All} reports the unweighted mean across duplication levels. }}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:piqa-mia}{{4}{6}{}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces  Cross benchmark transfer of the suppression direction. Rows are the corpus the direction was estimated on, columns the test set it was applied to. Each result reports \textbf  {accuracy\,/\,agreement} (\%), where agreement is measured against the standard model, and every entry is the unweighted mean across duplication levels. The shaded row is the model before editing. Bold marks the highest agreement per test set. Wikipedia is a passage corpus that shares no examples with any test set, so its row is transfer throughout; the diagonal entries are estimated on the test set they are scored on and are the in-domain reference rather than a transfer result. The step size is selected per entry on validation examples at duplication $0$ and ranges over $\{0.005, 0.01, 0.02\}$. }}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cross-benchmark-transfer}{{5}{7}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces  Suppression as the labeled spiking-data budget shrinks. Each budget is a percentage of every full duplication group; 40\% is the complete training split. Each budget fits its own diagonal-Fisher direction and selects its step $\eta $ on validation. Results are reported on the held-out test split as \textbf  {accuracy\,/\,agreement} (\%), with agreement measured against the standard model, and are unweighted means across duplication levels. The shaded row is the perturbed model before editing. }}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:spiking-data-budget}{{6}{7}{}{table.6}{}}
